### Rationale
This is a negative result. The capped-4 persistent grid was slower than the parent r5 one-shot kernel at every size on the rented B200, and I found no size where it wins. I tested four layouts, all correct on 16/16. Change vs r5 by band (S/M/L):

| Version | Layout | S | M | L |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| v1 | Q and K row pairs (one-step prefetch in practice) | +8% | +6% | +11% |
| v2 | same, true two-step ping-pong prefetch | +23% | +25% | +19% |
| v3 | rows of the joined [Q;K] stream, strided | +4% | +4% | +11% |
| v4 | each CTA owns a contiguous range of tiles | +5% | +9% | +0% (parent kernel for L) |

v2 had no register spills, yet more loads in flight made it slower. Narrowing the active address window helped (v1 → v3). Both point to DRAM access pattern or read/write mixing, not latency, as the limit. That is a guess I have not tested.

I am submitting v3's capped path for B·S ≤ 2048 and the parent's kernel above that. It was tested exactly as given: projected score 0.604, against 0.609 for the parent. It records the niche and should not replace r5.

